# executed by write_summaries.py (shares S, m, v, N)
S["ablationSummary"] = (r"No single component dominates: on the 100-customer instances all variants stay within $\pm0.05\%$ of the full algorithm, and on the 200-customer instance the means are governed by occasional runs that end in a solution with an additional vehicle. The number of runs that end more than $0.1\%$ above the best known value is the more stable indicator. It is " + m("abl_OSD_trapped") + r" of 25 for the full algorithm, " + m("abl_flatweights_trapped") + r" with flat sequence weights (Proposition~\ref{prop:backoff}) and " + m("abl_smallpool_trapped") + r" with the reduced $2\times2$ operator pool, which indicates the value of the hierarchical weights and of the enlarged pool. TWCC-GRH mainly accelerates the early search: with a randomized first-fit start the final solutions are of the same quality.")

S["lenSummary"] = (r"Table~\ref{tab:len} in Appendix~\ref{app:results} varies the sequence space on six 75-customer instances. All four variants lie within $0.02\%$ of the best mean and cost the same time per run. The default variable-length space ($L\leq2$) has the smallest average deviation (" + m("len_Lenle2_dev_all") + r"\% against " + m("len_Len1_dev_all") + r"\% for single pairs, " + m("len_Len2_dev_all") + r"\% for fixed pairs of length two and " + m("len_Lenle3_dev_all") + r"\% for $L\leq3$), although none of the differences is significant (Wilcoxon $p\geq" + m("len_wilcoxon_Len2") + r"$). Extending the space to sequences of length three (4\,368 sequences) neither helps nor hurts: with the hierarchical weights the size of the sequence space has no measurable cost, so $L_{\max}=2$ is retained as the simplest choice.")
S["usageSummary"] = (r"Both layers learn clear preferences among the pairs: the most frequent pair of ALNS-P is selected " + m("usage_Ptop_min") + "--" + m("usage_Ptop_max") + r" of the time (uniform: 0.063), and in both layers random removal followed by regret-2 or regret-3 insertion dominates, consistent with the ablation result that the enlarged pool matters. OSD-ALNS applies a sequence of two pairs in " + m("usage_share2_min") + "--" + m("usage_share2_max") + r" of its iterations, i.e.\ the length adaptation mixes single and compound moves and shifts the search moderately towards single moves, and its most frequent sequence is selected four to six times more often than under uniform selection.")
S["protoTSummary"] = (r"Under equal CPU time the three ALNS variants clearly dominate the two structurally different metaheuristics. VNS deviates from the best known solutions by " + m("pT_VNS_dev") + r"\% on average (up to " + m("pT_VNS_max") + r"\% on a single instance) and GA by " + m("pT_GA_dev") + r"\%, against " + m("pT_I_dev") + ", " + m("pT_P_dev") + " and " + m("pT_OSD_dev") + r"\% for ALNS-I, ALNS-P and OSD-ALNS. OSD-ALNS is better than VNS on " + m("pT_OSD_wins_VNS") + r" instances and worse on " + m("pT_OSD_losses_VNS") + r" (Wilcoxon $p<0.001$), and better than GA on " + m("pT_OSD_wins_GA") + r" instances and worse on none ($p<0.001$); the Friedman test over the five methods rejects equality ($p<0.001$) with mean ranks " + m("pT_rank_P") + " (ALNS-P), " + m("pT_rank_I") + " (ALNS-I), " + m("pT_rank_OSD") + " (OSD-ALNS), " + m("pT_rank_VNS") + " (VNS) and " + m("pT_rank_GA") + r" (GA), and the three ALNS variants do not differ significantly from each other. The VNS, whose local search re-inserts every request at its best position with the exact evaluation, is competitive on the small instances but falls behind on the larger ones, where the time budget allows only a few shaking cycles; the GA suffers from its indirect encoding, in which the split decoder cannot exploit the time-window structure. These results support the choice of the destroy--repair framework with exact evaluation for this problem.")

S["sensSummary"] = (r"Three patterns emerge. (a) The objective decreases with the radius factor $\gamma$: enlarging the region served by independent drones moves customers from the expensive truck-supported mode (a truck stop costs $c_T=50$ CNY plus travel at $c_t=0.083$ CNY/s) to the cheap independent mode ($c_D=10$ CNY per trip plus flight at $c_d=0.0017$ CNY/s), and because trucks may wait at no cost the time windows do not penalize this shift. The curve is a staircase, each step corresponding to a group of customers that becomes reachable: on the 15-customer instance the cost falls from " + m("sens_gamma_15_first") + r" CNY ($\gamma=0$, trucks only) to " + m("sens_gamma_15_min") + r" CNY at $\gamma\geq0.4$, when all customers are within the independent-drone radius, and on the 30-customer instance from " + m("sens_gamma_30_first") + " to " + m("sens_gamma_30_min") + r" CNY at $\gamma\geq0.45$, with small intermediate non-monotonicities ($340.5$ to $350.2$ CNY between $\gamma=0.2$ and $0.35$) caused by the repeated pre-processing. The U-shaped behaviour sometimes reported for the share of drone service does not appear here because the independent-drone fleet is sized to the demand and waiting is free; it would require a binding fleet or a cost of waiting. (b) Drone endurance exhibits strongly diminishing returns: up to $L_d=8$~km the cost stays high (small clusters, many sorties and many truck stops), between 8 and 10--11~km it falls by a quarter to a third, and beyond 11~km nothing changes because all customers are then reachable with the existing clusters at this spatial scale ($10\times10$~km$^2$). (c) Drone payload has an equally strong effect because the demand a cluster may hold is $\min\{Q_t,|L_1|Q_d\}$: raising $Q_d$ from 4 to 6 units reduces the cost of the 30-customer instance by $26\%$ and to $Q_d=12$ by $36\%$, whereas the 15-customer instance is nearly saturated from $Q_d=7$ on. The small non-monotonicities (e.g.\ $L_d=11$~km versus 10~km on the 30-customer instance) arise because the pre-processing is repeated for every value and the resulting partition is not guaranteed to be optimal, which shows that the clustering stage is itself a decision lever.")

S["conclVI"] = (r"On the benchmark the strengthening reduces the LP root gap from " + m("vi_toy_base_rootgap") + r"\% (toy) and " + m("vi_15_base_rootgap") + r"\% (15 customers) to " + m("vi_toy_all_rootgap") + r"\% and " + m("vi_15_all_rootgap") + r"\%, solves all toy instances within about one second instead of up to 48~s, and proves optimality on four of the six 15-customer instances where the base model proves none; the rounded fleet-size inequalities and the symmetry-breaking constraints are the decisive components.")

S["conclMgmt"] = (r"Under the cost structure of the study, in which a drone trip is an order of magnitude cheaper than a truck stop, three findings are relevant for operators. First, demand should be shifted to independent depot-based drones as far as their endurance allows; the truck-supported mode is the mode of last resort for customers beyond the independent-drone radius, and the planner should size the independent fleet to the demand rather than cap its share. Second, investments in endurance pay off only up to the distance at which every customer becomes reachable from its cluster---about 10--11~km on a $10\times10$~km$^2$ service area---and are wasted beyond it, whereas endurance below 8~km is costly because clusters fragment. Third, drone payload is at least as important as endurance in a multi-visit setting because it determines how much demand a single wave of sorties can serve from a truck stop; raising the payload from 4 to 6 units reduced the cost of the 30-customer instance by about a quarter. Finally, the clustering stage should be re-optimized whenever fleet parameters change, since a fixed partition can offset part of the benefit of better drones.")

S["conclAlgo"] = (r"OSD-ALNS reached the exact optimum on every small instance with a proven optimum, attained the smallest average gap to the reference values (" + m("pE_OSD_gap") + r"\% against " + m("pE_I_gap") + r"\% for conventional ALNS and " + m("pE_P_gap") + r"\% for pair-coupled ALNS) and was most effective on the clustered and random instances with 50 to 100 customers (for example " + m("pE_OSD_gap_C75") + r"\% against " + m("pE_I_gap_C75") + r"\% on the 75-customer C101 instances), where it keeps improving after the conventional layers have stalled. On the mixed RC101 instances and on the largest instances the three layers performed similarly. The ablation indicates the value of the hierarchical weights and of the enlarged operator pool. OSD-ALNS can therefore serve as a drop-in replacement for the adaptive layer of conventional ALNS: it requires no additional parameters or learning model, costs the same per operation, is non-inferior within $0.02$ percentage points over the whole benchmark and pays off on instances with a clustered or random structure of moderate size. Under equal CPU time all three ALNS variants clearly outperformed variable neighborhood search and a genetic algorithm, which underlines the value of the destroy--repair framework with exact evaluation for this problem.")
